{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cohen s d cohen s d","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md","fileName":"Cohen's d.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cohen's d","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 18:44\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Statistical Power and Effect Size]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Statistical Power and Effect Size","analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cohen s d cohen s d","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md","fileName":"Cohen's d.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cohen's d","heading":"Cohen's d","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Cohen's d is a standardized difference between two means, expressed relative to a measure of within-group variability. It puts differences measured on different continuous scales into comparable standard-deviation units.\n\nThe statistic can supply the effect argument for a two-sample power calculation. It should not be confused with the unstandardized difference in means, and the choice of pooled or alternative standardization must match the design and software function used.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Statistical Power and Effect Size","analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cohen s d cohen s d","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cohen's d.md","fileName":"Cohen's d.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cohen's d","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTit